PATCH 521: the [DGROUP:0x6b4] paint calls pass the position descriptor in ARGUMENT 4, not argument 2.

FUN_0000_26a1 -- the sprite blit dispatcher the [0x6b4] vector points at (vector-install table at MGA
image 0x5406, (slot, method) pairs: 0x06ac->0x25d1, 0x06b0->0x2604, 0x06b4->0x26a1, 0x06b8->0x2660) --
runs as `push bx ; mov di,[0x724] ; call 0x260c`, so AX (the sprite-directory byte offset) and BX (the
position descriptor) arrive LIVE from the caller.  Ghidra's __allregs varnode order places BX in 26a1's
param_4, and the call sites that predate this series already use exactly that shape:

    (*(code *)fist_icall_far((uint32_t)(DAT_1000_c6b4)))(sprite, 0, 0, rect_off, 0);

Patches 503-520 introduced 64 new call sites that passed `(ax, bx)` instead, so 26a1 read the descriptor
from param_4 -- stack garbage -- on every one of them.  All 64 are converted to the established shape.

HOW THE CONVENTION WAS ESTABLISHED, recorded because I got it backwards first.  My initial attempt
changed the CALLEE (26a1, and "for uniformity" 279d and 294d) to read param_2.  That broke every menu
and dialog screen: the native verify matrix went 177 PASS / 0 FAIL -> 1 PASS / 26 FAIL, mainmenu at
ref-AE=9675.  Restricting the change to 26a1 alone did not help either (tick 2264 instead of 5075, same
AE).  Breaking on 26a1 during the mainmenu flow shows why:

    26a1 p1=13d8 p2=0000 p3=0000 p4=f790 p5=0000
    #1  FUN_0000_e9f0     #2  FUN_0000_209e

the menu's own caller passes the descriptor 0xf790 in param_4 -- exactly the MAINMENU case patch 114's
header already documents ("for the MAINMENU box (ax=0x13d8, pos=0xf790) word[clip]==0xa000 => ZF=1 =>
the full path").  The callee was right all along; the new callers were wrong.

MEASURED: with the handlers fixed (patch 517) FUN_0000_787d hands 26a1 ax=0x380 / bx=0x8eee exactly as
the asm says, and before this patch 260c still received 0xe515.  A watchpoint capturing all eight writes
to DGROUP:0x158a inside tick 274 showed every one of that frame's 260c calls taking its descriptor from
the wrong slot.
